    [...]
> I agree with that: disenchanters are a pain in the neck (so
> are mind flayers).

Daggers or bow and arrow are effective against mind flayers.
Not recommended against disenchanters, however, unless you're
willing to loose some (or at least end up with two stacks, one
less enchanted).

> I chose to wear a CoMR instead of a robe in my current game so
> I can cast almost no spells.  Whenever disenchanters draw
> near, I have to rely on wands to dispell them.  However, they
> are so hard to kill with wands, so some did touch me and cause
> me a lot of inconvenience.

No harder to kill with wands that with spells.  CoMR has magic
cancellation of 3, I think, so the probability of their doing
any harm unless you attact them with a weapon (or in hand to
hand combat with gloves) is pretty small.

> I lost my +5 speed boots on my way up due to one stupid move:
> my CoMR was disenchanted to +3 and I wanted to re-enchant it.
> After using the "A" command to disrobe everything except CoMR,
> I failed to notice the resulting message was "You *stop*
> disrobing" instead of "You finish disrobing", and I read my
> b?oEA......presto! and the speed boots are gone!  You know
> what a slowed down samurai look like.

Samurai's are fast to begin with (although speed boots do speed
them up even more).  It would be worse with one of the other
roles.

> Speaking about the spells, I have 100% failure rate to almost all
> spells.  But I finally found that useful in my end game: I just could
> not find the magic portal in the Plane of Fire and my boots were badly
> burnt.  I just cast a difficult spell to confuse myself, and you know
> what happened.

That's not difficulty; that's because you've forgotten the
spell.  I'll always read all of the cheapest spellbooks very
early in the game, so that I'll have some useless forgotten
spell when I get to the planes.

